        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 1066,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Averages a set of orientations into the one that is, overall, closest to all of them.\n\nAveraging the four quaternion numbers directly goes wrong: q and -q are the same turn, so a set that happens to mix signs averages towards nonsense - towards zero, which is no rotation at all. scipy.rotation_mean is unaffected by signs. Use it to find a rest pose from a stretch of frames, a calibration orientation from repeated readings, a consensus of several sensors on one segment, or a typical orientation of a joint over a phrase.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.rotation_mean\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nquaternions:\nThe orientations, one row of w x y z each. Every input is averaged and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nweights:\nHow much each counts, held: one non-negative number per row. Without weights all count the same.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmean:\nThe average orientation, as w x y z.\n\nQUATERNIONS:\nFour numbers w x y z, scalar first - the same order as the quaternion nodes. A turn of an angle about an axis is w = cos(angle / 2) and x y z = the axis times sin(angle / 2): no turn is 1 0 0 0; a quarter turn about z is 0.7071 0 0 0.7071. q and -q are the same turn.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThree turns about z, of 10, 20 and 30 degrees - the middle one written with the opposite sign (-0.98 ... -0.17 instead of 0.98 ... 0.17), the same turn. Their mean is 20 degrees about z: 0.985 0 0 0.174. np.mean of the same numbers gives 0.326 0 0 0.057 - not even a proper rotation. A metro re-sends the set twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.slerp turns smoothly between orientations. np.mean averages ordinary values;\nscipy.circmean averages single angles.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1050,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Averages a set of orientations into the one that is, overall, closest to all of them.\n\nAveraging the four quaternion numbers directly goes wrong: q and -q are the same turn, so a set that happens to mix signs averages towards nonsense - towards zero, which is no rotation at all. scipy.rotation_mean is unaffected by signs. Use it to find a rest pose from a stretch of frames, a calibration orientation from repeated readings, a consensus of several sensors on one segment, or a typical orientation of a joint over a phrase.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.rotation_mean\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nquaternions:\nThe orientations, one row of w x y z each. Every input is averaged and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nweights:\nHow much each counts, held: one non-negative number per row. Without weights all count the same.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmean:\nThe average orientation, as w x y z.\n\nQUATERNIONS:\nFour numbers w x y z, scalar first - the same order as the quaternion nodes. A turn of an angle about an axis is w = cos(angle / 2) and x y z = the axis times sin(angle / 2): no turn is 1 0 0 0; a quarter turn about z is 0.7071 0 0 0.7071. q and -q are the same turn.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThree turns about z, of 10, 20 and 30 degrees - the middle one written with the opposite sign (-0.98 ... -0.17 instead of 0.98 ... 0.17), the same turn. Their mean is 20 degrees about z: 0.985 0 0 0.174. np.mean of the same numbers gives 0.326 0 0 0.057 - not even a proper rotation. A metro re-sends the set twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.slerp turns smoothly between orientations. np.mean averages ordinary values;\nscipy.circmean averages single angles."
        },
